> [!WARNING]
> **`calendarDays` copies the whole day map once per incident day, so the render goes quadratic**
>
> <sub>F9 · Warning · performance</sub>

The inner `reduce` starts from `acc` and returns `new Map(acc)` for every day of every entry, so building the calendar copies the entire day map (up to 365 keys) once per incident-day. With the 500-entry index the builder allows, 500 incidents each spanning about 300 days took 3.7 s of CPU when I ran this algorithm standalone (200 entries x 200 days: 660 ms), and it runs in `renderPage` for every visitor. Accumulate into the map the reduce already owns instead of cloning it.

```suggestion
^return entries.reduce((days, entry, index) => {
		const first = utcDay(entry.startedAt) < oldest ? oldest : utcDay(entry.startedAt)
		const endDay = utcDay(Math.min(entry.resolvedAt ?? nowMs, nowMs))
		const span = Math.max(0, Math.round((dayMs(endDay) - dayMs(first)) / DAY_MS)) + 1
		for (let offset = 0; offset < span; offset += 1) {
			const day = shiftDay(first, offset)
			if (day > today) continue
			const current = days.get(day)
			const tone =
				current === undefined || TONE_RANK[entry.tone] > TONE_RANK[current.tone]
					? entry.tone
					: current.tone
			days.set(day, { tone, entries: [...(current?.entries ?? []), index] })
		}
		return days
	}, new Map<string, CalendarDay>())
```
